Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=('2571bda3e481b9bdf388c8479e908017285c95163b688a13ce33678a92387aef',), event=Revised(decision='train', at=datetime(2026, 11, 6, 0, 0, 0), actor='assistant', title='The merge queue is scripts/train.sh; reviewed PRs ride a train and nobody merges a train by hand', body="*The merge queue is* `scripts/train.sh`: reviewed pull requests ride a TRAIN. `build <pr>...` merges them onto `train` from `origin/master` in an order that is a function of their heads: a PR whose head holds more of the others' merges first, so a stacked PR's merge carries its base, and the head's name orders the rest. A PR's changelog entry is its own fragment, `changelog.d/<branch>.md`, and the release cut folds the fragments into the version's section and deletes them, so no two PRs edit one hunk; `app/Cargo.lock` keeps the train's side and cargo adds the PR's; any other conflict is skipped for its author. `gate --remote github` runs the tree half on core's runners (the train commit's public tree, once its leak guard passes, pushed to core's `gate/<train sha>`) and the live half on the box, keeping each verdict in `.git/train/` keyed by half and tree: a tree both green and red is a flaky gate, and lands nothing. `nightly` runs both halves in full on master. `bisect` finds the merge that turned a half red. `land` pushes master only from a HEAD both halves passed, then publishes it; `scripts/deploy.sh <rev>` is the operator's. A STACKED PR branches from its base PR's branch and lands with it or after it. Nobody merges a train by hand.", rationale="Decided 2026-10-03: GitHub's merge queue is for organisations, and the gate is the box's. Revised 2026-11-06: the changelog is fragments (#360), the Codespace remote is gone (#336), and the nightly full gate exists.", supersedes=()))